   "desc": "Chicago AI, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup Join our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics across MCP, agents and skills. **Date, Time and Location** Feb 25, 2027 9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST Online. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/mcp-agents-skills-meetup-february-25-2027)** **Your Agent Crashed After a Tool Call. Now What?** Agent demos rarely show what happens when the worker dies halfway through a tool workflow. I’ll demonstrate crash recovery with LangGraph and Pydantic AI using Azure Durable Task Scheduler. I’ll show persisted progress, retries, and a human approval step. I’ll distinguish workflow recovery from external effects: preventing duplicate effects requires idempotency enforced by the external API. *About the Speaker* [Tamir Dresher](https://www.linkedin.com/in/tamirdresher/) is a Principal Engineer on Microsoft's Azure Serverless team. Tamir built the official Azure Durable Task Scheduler integrations for LangGraph and Pydantic AI. **Before You Connect That MCP Server: What It Can Reach, What It Can Do** Connect an agent to an MCP server and that server gets access to your credentials and a standing invitation to act for you. Independent researcher Syed Anas Mohiuddin is credited on 16 GitHub security advisories for MCP servers, covering server-side request forgery, command injection, missing authentication, session hijack, and credential leaks. This talk goes through the checks to run before you connect a server: what it can reach on the network, what it stores and logs, and which of its tools can change real systems. It then covers the check that has to sit at run time, between the agent and the action, so a poisoned tool description or a forged request meets a person before it reaches production. Attendees get a short list of questions to put to any MCP server they already run. *About the Speaker* [Michael Kantor](https://www.linkedin.com/in/kantorcodes) is President of HOL, a nonprofit consortium that builds open standards for AI agent infrastructure. HOL's product is HOL Guard, a firewall for any AI agent, which checks risky actions before they run. On 10 November 2026 he spoke online at [re]aligned, a CanSecWest extension, on testing runtime controls for agents: \"Did the Agent Guard Stop the Action, or Only the Words?\" **Flash Demo: Agents in Action with Voxel51** In this flash demo, Adonai will show some of the agent workflows the Voxel51 team has been building, and how you can put agents to work on your own visual AI data with Voxel51. Expect a quick, hands-on look at what's possible today and how to try it yourself. *About the Speaker* [Adonai Vera](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-vera/) is a Machine Learning Engineer and DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 years of experience building computer vision and machine learning models using TensorFlow, Docker, and OpenCV.",
